SECTION 1: FACTUAL SUMMARY
(System access records -- Ellingson IT log, confirmed)

Wallace, Margo accessed the Gibson mainframe at 11:42 PM on October 15
using credentials provisioned by Eugene Belford on October 12. The
access session lasted 47 minutes. During this session, three file writes
were recorded to the financial batch processing directory:

  /gibson/finance/batch/dvinci.exe   written 11:44 PM
  /gibson/finance/batch/dvinci.cfg   written 11:51 PM
  /gibson/finance/cron/nightly.sh    modified 11:58 PM (added dvinci exec)

The Da Vinci executable ran for the first time at 2:00 AM on October 16
as part of the nightly batch. It ran undetected for 18 hours before
Sarah Chen flagged anomalous transaction patterns on October 16 at
8:14 PM. Total transactions affected: 4,182. Estimated diversion:
$1,047.23 (salami logic -- fractional amounts per transaction).

Secret Service seized the Gibson on October 17. Forensic imaging is
confirmed. FBI has been notified.

---

SECTION 2: RECOMMENDED ACTIONS

Hal, given the above, here is our assessment and recommended next steps.

Your exposure as CEO is limited but real. The key question is what you
knew and when. Our recommendation is that you do not make any statements
to investigators -- Secret Service or FBI -- without counsel present.
This applies to informal conversations as well.

Specifically: do not discuss Belford's role in provisioning Wallace's
credentials. That chain of events will come out in discovery, and any
characterisation you offer now will be used to establish what you knew.
Let the documents speak for themselves.

We recommend retaining outside criminal counsel immediately -- this
situation is beyond the scope of our corporate engagement. We can make
introductions if helpful.

Do not destroy, alter, or quarantine any documents or communications
related to this matter. Preservation obligations are now in effect.

We will advise further as the situation develops.

-- Ellingson Legal

